window.CV_PRACTICAL_DATA = [{"id":"01","slug":"setup","title":"Your local vision lab","stage":"Start here","time":"1–2 hours","lessons":[1,7,40],"summary":"Read a tensor, inspect a batch and set up a reproducible local Python environment.","file":"labs/01-setup.md","checks":["Environment check passes and versions are recorded","I can explain NCHW, RGB, float32 and the label dtype","I can run a module from the repository root"],"html":"<p>The browser teaches the calculations. These labs use installed Python to read files, train models and save results. You can start this lab before completing the maths course. Python basics to know: variables, loops, functions, lists, imports and indexing; use the <a href=\"https://cs231n.github.io/python-numpy-tutorial/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CS231n Python/NumPy tutorial ↗<\/a> to refresh them.<\/p>\n<h2>Set up once<\/h2>\n<p>If you are using the hosted course, first download and extract the <a href=\"https://github.com/jatin711-debug/maths-for-computer-vision\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">repository ↗<\/a>, or clone it:<\/p>\n<pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>git clone https://github.com/jatin711-debug/maths-for-computer-vision.git\ncd maths-for-computer-vision<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Open a terminal in the repository root, the folder containing <code>README.md<\/code> and <code>course<\/code>. Create a Python 3.10–3.12 environment and install the practical requirements. On Windows:<\/p>\n<pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>python -m venv course/practical/.venv\ncourse/practical/.venv/Scripts/python.exe -m pip install -r course/practical/requirements.txt\ncourse/practical/.venv/Scripts/python.exe -m course.practical.check_environment<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Activate with <code>course/practical/.venv/Scripts/Activate.ps1<\/code>, or replace <code>python<\/code> in subsequent commands with the full environment executable. On macOS/Linux use <code>python3 -m venv course/practical/.venv<\/code>, then <code>source course/practical/.venv/bin/activate<\/code> and <code>python -m pip install -r course/practical/requirements.txt<\/code>. The setup guide is downloadable from the practical overview.<\/p>\n<h2>Understand the first batch<\/h2>\n<p>Run this in a Python session in your environment:<\/p>\n<pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>import torch\nfrom PIL import Image\nfrom torchvision.transforms import functional as F\nimage = Image.new(&quot;RGB&quot;, (48, 32), (255, 128, 0))\nx = F.to_tensor(image)\nbatch = torch.stack([x, x])\nlabels = torch.tensor([0, 1], dtype=torch.long)\nprint(x.shape, x.dtype, x[:, 0, 0])\nprint(batch.shape, labels.shape, labels.dtype)<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Predict before running: Pillow&#x27;s size is width then height; a tensor is channels, height, width. The image becomes <code>(3,32,48)<\/code>, the batch <code>(2,3,32,48)<\/code>, and labels <code>(2,)<\/code>. The orange pixel becomes roughly <code>(1,0.502,0)<\/code>. Pixel floats are scaled to 0–1; labels remain integer class indices. Adding a batch dimension is different from adding a colour channel.<\/p>\n<p>OpenCV conventionally uses BGR arrays; Pillow and the neural network paths here use RGB. Reversing red and blue can silently damage a trained model. <code>model.eval()<\/code> changes dropout/normalization behavior; <code>torch.inference_mode()<\/code> turns off gradient bookkeeping. They serve different purposes.<\/p>\n<h2>Make a reproducible workspace<\/h2>\n<p>Downloaded datasets live in <code>course/practical/data<\/code>; outputs in <code>course/practical/runs<\/code>. Both are ignored by Git. Each experiment gets a new directory. Save your Python/package versions and the complete command. CPU is the default; passing <code>--device cuda<\/code> requires a compatible CUDA installation. Start small before increasing data or model size.<\/p>\n<h2>Check your understanding<\/h2>\n<p>Change the image to grayscale and predict the channel dimension. Explain why a classification target is not a float image. Explain why a successful import is different from a successful training run. Save the environment output and tensor observations in your lab notes.<\/p>\n<h2>If something fails<\/h2>\n<p><code>No module named course<\/code> means you are probably outside the repository root. A missing <code>torchvision::nms<\/code> operator usually indicates incompatible torch/torchvision builds; reinstall them as a matched pair using the <a href=\"https://pytorch.org/get-started/locally/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">official installer ↗<\/a>. Reduce batch size on out-of-memory errors. First downloads require internet; the original maths course remains usable offline.<\/p>"},{"id":"02","slug":"opencv","title":"Count objects with OpenCV","stage":"Images and data","time":"2–3 hours","lessons":[1,33,34,57],"summary":"Turn a photograph into a binary mask and a measured object count.","file":"labs/02-opencv.md","checks":["Saved the source photo, mask and annotated count","Compared two area/kernel settings against a manual count","Explained a merged-object or illumination failure"],"html":"<p>Photograph several separated coins, beans or dark buttons on a plain contrasting background. Save it as <code>course/practical/data/objects.jpg<\/code>. Count them yourself first and record the number. Lighting, shadows and touching objects will become explicit assumptions of your algorithm.<\/p>\n<h2>Build the pipeline<\/h2>\n<pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>python -m course.practical.opencv_lab count --image course/practical/data/objects.jpg --dark --min-area 80 --kernel 3 --run course/practical/runs/count-a<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Use <code>--dark<\/code> for dark objects on a light background; omit it for the reverse. The script converts BGR to grayscale, selects an Otsu threshold, opens the binary mask with a small kernel, and labels connected components. It removes components smaller than <code>--min-area<\/code>. It saves <code>mask.png<\/code>, <code>objects.jpg<\/code> and <code>counts.json<\/code>.<\/p>\n<p>Otsu chooses a threshold from the intensity histogram. It does not know what a coin is. Opening is erosion followed by dilation: it can remove tiny isolated foreground specks, but also erase narrow real objects. A component is a connected foreground region; two touching coins may form one region. The box is measured in image pixels, not physical centimetres.<\/p>\n<h2>Experiment<\/h2>\n<p>Repeat into <code>count-b<\/code> using <code>--kernel 5<\/code>, keeping everything else fixed. Compare the saved masks and count error against your manual count. Next change only <code>--min-area<\/code>. Which false positives disappear? Which true objects are lost? Photograph the same arrangement with a shadow, then with two objects touching. Keep your chosen settings fixed on those new photos.<\/p>\n<p>The area threshold depends on resolution. If each image dimension doubles, an object&#x27;s area grows by about four. Inspect colour spaces too: an HSV mask may separate a coloured object better than grayscale, but hue wraps around its numeric range. Try <code>cv2.cvtColor(image, cv2.COLOR_BGR2HSV)<\/code> and inspect values before choosing a range.<\/p>\n<h2>Evidence and mastery<\/h2>\n<p>Save three photo/mask/output triples, manual versus automatic counts, and one failure explanation. Distinguish “the threshold missed a dark object” from “the model does not understand coins.” State one scene condition under which this simple pipeline is sufficient. Explain why a neural network might be unnecessary for a controlled scene.<\/p>\n<p>Reference: <a href=\"https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d9/d61/tutorial_py_morphological_ops.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OpenCV morphology tutorial ↗<\/a> and <a href=\"https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d7/da8/tutorial_table_of_content_imgproc.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">image-processing tutorials ↗<\/a>.<\/p>"},{"id":"03","slug":"data","title":"Build and audit your dataset","stage":"Images and data","time":"3–4 hours","lessons":[16,28,41,47],"summary":"Write label rules, inspect duplicates and split independent groups before training.","file":"labs/03-data.md","checks":["Wrote label definitions and source/group metadata","Resolved corrupt images, duplicates and missing groups","Saved a fixed manifest and inspected class counts in every split"],"html":"<p>Pick two or three visually meaningful classes, such as mugs versus bottles. Start with at least 20 independently collected photos per class. Ten neighbouring video frames are not ten independent scenes. Record where each image came from and whether you can use it for the intended project.<\/p>\n<h2>Write an annotation contract<\/h2>\n<p>Before labeling, write what each class includes/excludes, how to handle uncertain examples, and whether multiple objects can coexist. For boxes, specify continuous <code>xyxy<\/code> boundaries and whether partially occluded objects get the visible or estimated full box. For masks, distinguish background, foreground, individual instances and any ignored pixels. Have a second person label a small sample if possible; otherwise label it twice at different times and review disagreements.<\/p>\n<p>Put classification photos in <code>course/practical/data/my-images/mug/<\/code> and <code>course/practical/data/my-images/bottle/<\/code>. Create <code>groups.csv<\/code> with one row per image:<\/p>\n<pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>path,group\nmug/desk-01.jpg,desk-session-1\nmug/desk-02.jpg,desk-session-1\nbottle/kitchen-01.jpg,kitchen-session-1<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Use the same group for the same subject, video, capture session or source whenever that relationship could make images unusually similar. The example above is just the schema; include every image and enough independent groups to represent every class in each split.<\/p>\n<h2>Audit before splitting<\/h2>\n<pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>python -m course.practical.data course/practical/data/my-images --groups course/practical/data/groups.csv --out course/practical/data/audit.json<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Inspect unreadable files, dimensions, class counts and duplicate groups in the JSON. The hash compares decoded, EXIF-oriented RGB pixels; it catches identical images with different file encodings, but not crops or near-duplicates. Review thumbnails for near-duplicates and labeling errors. Resolve duplicates manually and preserve an audit note; the script does not delete photographs.<\/p>\n<pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>python -m course.practical.data course/practical/data/my-images --groups course/practical/data/groups.csv --split --seed 7 --out course/practical/data/my-split.json<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>This assigns entire groups to approximately 60% train, 20% validation and 20% test. Fractions refer to groups, so image counts can differ. Missing classes trigger an error: improve coverage rather than repeatedly searching seeds until your favourite model scores well. Without a group CSV each image is considered independent; use that only when it is a defensible assumption.<\/p>\n<h2>Use the fixed split<\/h2>\n<pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>python -m course.practical.classify train --manifest course/practical/data/my-split.json --epochs 5 --run course/practical/runs/my-classifier<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The classifier verifies the manifest fingerprint, class order, duplicate hashes and group independence before loading a split, then verifies that split&#x27;s image pixels. Rebuild the audited manifest if the data or labels change. Augmentation belongs only in training and must preserve your class meaning. The scripts use fixed ImageNet normalization constants; any statistics you estimate yourself must come only from training images. Group IDs never become model inputs.<\/p>\n<h2>Evidence and mastery<\/h2>\n<p>Save the label contract, group CSV, audit and split manifest. Count each class in every split and explain how a random image split could inflate results. List three ambiguous examples and your decisions. Preserve the original test split while experimenting. The report template on Lab 12 includes this data contract.<\/p>"},{"id":"04","slug":"classifier","title":"Train a real-image classifier","stage":"Train and evaluate","time":"3–4 hours","lessons":[27,30,38,40,41],"summary":"Train a small CNN on CIFAR-10, compare a baseline and inspect confident errors.","file":"labs/04-classifier.md","checks":["Saved a protocol, learning history and validation predictions","Compared accuracy and class recall with the majority baseline","Explained three failures and kept the test set untouched"],"html":"<p>This lab uses CIFAR-10: ten categories of small real photographs. It provides the official train/test boundary; we split the training portion into train and validation, stratified by class. The test portion is never used during training. The small CNN has two convolutions and global averaging so you can trace every shape.<\/p>\n<h2>Run a small experiment<\/h2>\n<pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>python -m course.practical.classify train --download --per-class 50 --epochs 3 --run course/practical/runs/cifar-small<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The first command downloads the full archive (about 170 MB), even though it uses 50 images per class in each development split. Omit <code>--download<\/code> thereafter. <code>--per-class<\/code> is a speed control, not a representative full benchmark. No particular accuracy is promised from three epochs on this small subset.<\/p>\n<p>Open <code>classify.py<\/code> and follow <code>dataset<\/code>, <code>ClassificationData.__getitem__<\/code>, the <code>DataLoader<\/code>, forward pass, cross-entropy, <code>backward<\/code> and optimizer step. The network emits ten logits. Do not apply softmax before <code>cross_entropy<\/code>: that function combines the needed log-softmax and loss stably. <code>zero_grad<\/code> prevents gradients accumulating across batches accidentally.<\/p>\n<h2>Inspect the result<\/h2>\n<p><code>protocol.json<\/code> records exact image IDs, split hash, settings, versions and majority-class validation baseline. <code>history.json<\/code> records train loss and validation loss/accuracy. <code>validation.json<\/code> gives the confusion matrix, per-class recall and individual predictions. <code>failures.jpg<\/code> shows up to twelve confident mistakes if errors occurred. <code>best.pt<\/code> was chosen by validation accuracy; <code>last.pt<\/code> supports resume.<\/p>\n<p>The baseline selects the most common training class and predicts it for every validation image. Balanced accuracy averages recall over represented classes; ordinary accuracy averages correctness over images. On balanced CIFAR subsets they coincide, but they need not on your custom data.<\/p>\n<pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>python -m course.practical.report course/practical/runs/cifar-small<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Open the generated <code>report.html<\/code> to inspect learning curves and the confusion matrix. Choose three errors and look at their source photographs. Distinguish plausible ambiguity from a preprocessing mistake. A high softmax score does not prove calibrated confidence.<\/p>\n<h2>Extend one thing<\/h2>\n<p>Repeat with <code>--augment<\/code> and a new run directory, keeping the seed, subset, epochs and architecture fixed. Horizontal flips are acceptable for these categories, but not automatically for text or left/right labels. Write your hypothesis before comparing validation results. Save test evaluation for the final selected procedure in Lab 12.<\/p>\n<p>Reference: <a href=\"https://www.cs.toronto.edu/~kriz/cifar.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CIFAR-10 source ↗<\/a> and <a href=\"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/basics/data_tutorial.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PyTorch data tutorial ↗<\/a>.<\/p>"},{"id":"05","slug":"transfer","title":"Transfer learning and fine-tuning","stage":"Train and evaluate","time":"3–4 hours","lessons":[32,39,41,55],"summary":"Train a pretrained ResNet head, then unfreeze it for a controlled fine-tuning experiment.","file":"labs/05-transfer.md","checks":["Completed a frozen-head experiment on the fixed split","Completed fine-tuning initialized from the frozen model","Compared validation results and explained frozen BatchNorm"],"html":"<p>A pretrained model brings features learned on another dataset. We replace ResNet18&#x27;s final classifier with ten outputs, then compare two adaptation strategies on the same CIFAR split. The first run downloads about 45 MB of ImageNet weights. Images are resized to 224 × 224 and normalized with the saved ImageNet constants. Upsampling CIFAR cannot create missing detail.<\/p>\n<h2>Train only the head<\/h2>\n<pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>python -m course.practical.classify train --arch resnet18 --pretrained --freeze --per-class 50 --epochs 3 --run course/practical/runs/resnet-head<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>All backbone weights have <code>requires_grad=False<\/code>. The script also keeps the frozen backbone in evaluation mode, preventing BatchNorm running statistics from drifting. Only the new classification head learns. Freezing gradients alone would not achieve that behavior.<\/p>\n<h2>Unfreeze for a new experiment<\/h2>\n<pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>python -m course.practical.classify train --arch resnet18 --init course/practical/runs/resnet-head/best.pt --per-class 50 --epochs 3 --lr 0.0001 --run course/practical/runs/resnet-finetune<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>This initializes a fresh optimizer from the earlier best weights and trains all layers. It is a new fine-tuning stage, not a resumed identical experiment. The smaller learning rate reduces how quickly useful pretrained features are overwritten. Compare validation loss, accuracy, class recall and actual failures; an extra stage costs extra training, so record that budget.<\/p>\n<h2>Compare honestly<\/h2>\n<p>Compare the small CNN, frozen-head ResNet and fine-tuned ResNet. Preserve the data split and report differences in resolution, pretrained data, parameter count and training budget. This comparison answers a practical method-choice question, not a claim that one architecture is intrinsically better under equal conditions. Test your own data using the manifest option from Lab 03.<\/p>\n<h2>Evidence and mastery<\/h2>\n<p>Save both protocols, reports and three changed predictions. Explain which parameters update in each strategy and why pretraining can help with few labels. Describe a domain where ImageNet features might transfer poorly. Do not claim that a pretrained model has never seen related evaluation data without checking its provenance.<\/p>\n<p>Reference: <a href=\"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/beginner/transfer_learning_tutorial.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">official transfer-learning tutorial ↗<\/a>.<\/p>"},{"id":"06","slug":"diagnostics","title":"Debug and resume training","stage":"Train and evaluate","time":"2–3 hours","lessons":[20,32,39,46],"summary":"Use learning curves, reproducible resume and one-change experiments to diagnose training.","file":"labs/06-diagnostics.md","checks":["Rendered a learning-curve report and interpreted the gap","Resumed last.pt with matching settings","Ran one controlled comparison and recorded its limitation"],"html":"<p>When training looks wrong, inspect evidence in order: input pixels and labels, model outputs, loss, gradients, optimizer settings, then generalization. A large model does not fix mislabeled or incorrectly normalized inputs.<\/p>\n<h2>Read the curves<\/h2>\n<pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>python -m course.practical.report course/practical/runs/cifar-small<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>If both losses stay high, inspect labels and learning rate; the model may also lack useful capacity. If training loss falls while validation loss rises, investigate overfitting or a split mismatch. If results jump wildly, examine learning rate, small validation counts and accidental training-mode evaluation. Curves are diagnostic clues rather than unique explanations.<\/p>\n<h2>Resume an interrupted run<\/h2>\n<pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>python -m course.practical.classify train --per-class 50 --epochs 5 --resume course/practical/runs/cifar-small/last.pt --run course/practical/runs/cifar-small<\/code><\/pre>\n<p><code>--epochs 5<\/code> means five epochs total. Keep the original seed, architecture, learning rate, batch size, augmentation and freeze flags. A protocol mismatch is rejected. <code>last.pt<\/code> restores the optimizer, model, loader generator and torch RNG state; <code>best.pt<\/code> is an inference artifact and cannot resume training. Exact repeatability is tested on CPU within one environment. CUDA and different library/hardware versions may still differ.<\/p>\n<h2>Make failures informative<\/h2>\n<p>Use a tiny training subset to test whether the network can reduce training loss. If it cannot, inspect a batch and the class indices before increasing model size. This debug subset is not your validation/test evidence. Intentionally raise the learning rate for a separate short run, then restore it. Save the curves and explain what changed.<\/p>\n<p>Choose just one proper comparison: augmentation, learning rate or training budget. Use separate run directories with the same split seed. Repeat your chosen procedure with additional training seeds for a capstone, recording that a changed seed also changes the built-in CIFAR development split; use a custom fixed manifest if you need seed-only comparisons on identical data.<\/p>\n<h2>CPU/GPU and memory<\/h2>\n<p>Batch size trades memory for throughput. Start with a correct full-precision run. Automatic mixed precision is an optional extension after measuring a CUDA bottleneck; it requires autocast and gradient scaling, and these teaching scripts intentionally do not enable it. Device mismatch errors usually mean an input, target or model remained on the other device.<\/p>\n<h2>Evidence and mastery<\/h2>\n<p>Save one curve diagnosis, a resumed history and one controlled comparison. Explain why validation is not a training gradient source, why evaluation mode matters, and why changing a split after seeing errors weakens your conclusion.<\/p>"},{"id":"07","slug":"detection","title":"Detect people in photographs","stage":"Beyond classification","time":"4–6 hours","lessons":[42,44,56,65],"summary":"Fine-tune a detector, inspect boxes and compute explicitly defined AP50.","file":"labs/07-detection.md","checks":["Verified boxes and masks before training","Saved trained weights, AP50 and prediction overlays","Explained duplicate detections, missing objects and the AP convention"],"html":"<p>Detection predicts a variable number of objects. This lab fine-tunes a Faster R-CNN with a MobileNet backbone on PennFudan. There is one foreground class, person, indexed 1; 0 is background. Instance masks provide the ground-truth boxes. This is an educational dataset and split, not a comprehensive pedestrian benchmark.<\/p>\n<h2>Inspect the targets first<\/h2>\n<pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>python -m course.practical.dense train --task detect --download --pretrained --limit 8 --size 128 --epochs 1 --run course/practical/runs/detect-smoke<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>This first run downloads the roughly 54 MB dataset and roughly 75 MB pretrained detector. Eight images per development split and a small resolution verify the path. Before trusting any metric, inspect the green ground-truth boxes and red predictions in <code>overlays<\/code>. In <code>data.py<\/code>, each nonzero instance ID becomes a mask and a continuous-boundary box. Width is <code>x2-x1<\/code>; the maximum occupied pixel contributes its full area via <code>max(x)+1<\/code>.<\/p>\n<p>The DataLoader uses lists because photographs and object counts differ. During training, torchvision returns a dictionary of classification, box-regression and proposal losses. During evaluation it returns boxes, scores and labels. Model evaluation also uses its configured score filtering and non-maximum suppression.<\/p>\n<h2>Run the full development split<\/h2>\n<pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>python -m course.practical.dense train --task detect --pretrained --size 320 --epochs 5 --lr 0.0001 --augment --run course/practical/runs/detect-full<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Add <code>--device cuda<\/code> if available. This is slower than the classifier on CPU; the smoke run is the place to establish memory and time expectations. The optional flip updates images, masks and boxes together. Use a new run directory each time; dense training does not implement resume.<\/p>\n<h2>Read AP correctly<\/h2>\n<p><code>metrics.json<\/code> reports all-points interpolated AP at IoU .50 for one class. Predictions are globally ranked by score; each ground-truth box can match only one prediction. A duplicate prediction is a false positive. AP uses all detections returned by the model; the .5 score cutoff used to draw red boxes affects only the illustrations. The metric is not COCO AP averaged over .50:.95, and it ignores none of the objects as crowd regions because this dataset path does not define crowds.<\/p>\n<p>Compare a high-score false positive, an occluded missed person and a poorly localized box. Threshold selection is a validation decision. AP summarizes a ranking; a deployed threshold still needs its own precision/recall tradeoff.<\/p>\n<h2>Final evaluation, after choosing settings<\/h2>\n<pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>python -m course.practical.dense evaluate --task detect --checkpoint course/practical/runs/detect-full/best.pt --split test --run course/practical/runs/detect-test<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The checkpoint restores the original size and split seed. Training never evaluates test images. PennFudan lacks reliable identity/group metadata for this workflow: related people or scenes may cross the image split. State that limitation explicitly.<\/p>\n<h2>Evidence and mastery<\/h2>\n<p>Save the protocol, best weights, AP curve arrays and three annotated failures. Explain why one-object localization is simpler than detection, how NMS differs from evaluation matching, and how small objects suffer when you lower resolution.<\/p>\n<p>Reference: <a href=\"https://docs.pytorch.org/tutorials/intermediate/torchvision_tutorial.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Torchvision detection tutorial ↗<\/a> and <a href=\"https://www.cis.upenn.edu/~jshi/ped_html/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PennFudan source ↗<\/a>.<\/p>"},{"id":"08","slug":"segmentation","title":"Segment people pixel by pixel","stage":"Beyond classification","time":"3–5 hours","lessons":[43,44,56],"summary":"Train a small U-Net and compare foreground overlap against an all-background baseline.","file":"labs/08-segmentation.md","checks":["Verified categorical masks and joint spatial transforms","Compared foreground IoU/Dice with the background baseline","Inspected boundary errors and explained semantic versus instance masks"],"html":"<p>Use the same PennFudan photographs with a different output: foreground person versus background for every pixel. We merge all nonzero instance IDs into one semantic foreground class. This loses information about which person is which, deliberately.<\/p>\n<h2>Train a small U-Net<\/h2>\n<pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>python -m course.practical.dense train --task segment --download --limit 8 --size 128 --epochs 2 --run course/practical/runs/segment-smoke\npython -m course.practical.dense train --task segment --size 128 --epochs 10 --augment --run course/practical/runs/segment-full<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The network encodes high-resolution features, pools to a coarser level, upsamples and concatenates the earlier features before predicting one logit per pixel. Read <code>TinyUNet.forward<\/code> and name each shape. Binary cross-entropy with logits trains the model; a sigmoid threshold of .5 defines the reported mask.<\/p>\n<p>RGB images use bilinear resizing; categorical masks use nearest-neighbour resizing. Interpolating IDs would invent meaningless classes. Every spatial flip must also flip the target. The teaching path resizes to a square, which can distort shapes; record it as an assumption and compare an aspect-preserving strategy as a later extension.<\/p>\n<h2>Read the outputs<\/h2>\n<p><code>metrics.json<\/code> contains pooled pixel counts, foreground IoU, foreground Dice and pixel accuracy. It also reports an all-background baseline on exactly the evaluated images. If both predicted and true foreground are absent everywhere, foreground overlap is undefined and saved as null. Green overlay pixels are true foreground, red false foreground, blue missed foreground.<\/p>\n<p>Inspect thin limbs, touching people, boundaries and clutter. A high pixel accuracy can coexist with nearly zero foreground recall because most pixels are background. Dice and IoU reward overlap differently; neither is a substitute for visual inspection. Thresholded metrics differ from a differentiable soft-Dice training objective.<\/p>\n<h2>Experiment<\/h2>\n<p>Compare the fixed full run with one change: horizontal augmentation or image resolution. Keep the split and epoch budget recorded. If you add a class-weighted loss, choose its weight from training data only. Keep the .5 threshold fixed unless you explicitly select a new threshold on validation and record it with the artifact.<\/p>\n<h2>Final evaluation<\/h2>\n<pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>python -m course.practical.dense evaluate --task segment --checkpoint course/practical/runs/segment-full/best.pt --split test --run course/practical/runs/segment-test<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>Run this only after choosing the method. The same image-split limitations as the detection lab apply.<\/p>\n<h2>Evidence and mastery<\/h2>\n<p>Save three overlays, foreground metrics, the all-background baseline and one boundary failure analysis. Explain why a resized categorical mask must remain discrete and why this model cannot give separate IDs to overlapping people. Review Lessons 43, 44 and 56 when connecting the code to the loss and architecture.<\/p>"},{"id":"09","slug":"geometry","title":"Align views and track motion","stage":"Beyond classification","time":"3–4 hours","lessons":[50,52,58,59,60],"summary":"Estimate a robust homography and track image features through a short video.","file":"labs/09-geometry.md","checks":["Saved inlier matches, a warp and reprojection errors","Saved a tracked video and feature survival statistics","Explained parallax, occlusion and why feature tracks are not object IDs"],"html":"<p>Take two overlapping photographs of a flat poster or book cover from different viewpoints. Save them as <code>view-a.jpg<\/code> and <code>view-b.jpg<\/code> inside <code>course/practical/data<\/code>. Use texture and good lighting; a blank wall cannot supply reliable feature matches.<\/p>\n<h2>Estimate a homography<\/h2>\n<pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>python -m course.practical.opencv_lab match --image course/practical/data/view-a.jpg --second course/practical/data/view-b.jpg --run course/practical/runs/alignment<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>ORB finds keypoints and binary descriptors. Hamming distance compares descriptors. The ratio test rejects a match when the second-best candidate is almost as good as the best; ambiguous repeated patterns are risky. RANSAC seeks a homography supported by a consistent subset of matches. The matrix maps coordinates in the first photo into the second.<\/p>\n<p>Inspect <code>matches.jpg<\/code>, <code>warped.jpg<\/code> and <code>geometry.json<\/code>. Record the inlier fraction and median inlier reprojection error in pixels. Low residual on the fitted inliers does not prove a correct whole-image alignment. Check corners or points you did not use to fit it. A homography is appropriate for a plane or near-pure camera rotation; translating around a 3D scene introduces parallax.<\/p>\n<h2>Track features in video<\/h2>\n<p>Record a short video of a textured object moving slowly. Save a local video file as <code>motion.mp4<\/code>; this script never opens the webcam.<\/p>\n<pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>python -m course.practical.opencv_lab track --video course/practical/data/motion.mp4 --max-frames 150 --run course/practical/runs/motion<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The script detects corners in the first frame, tracks them with pyramidal Lucas–Kanade, and checks that backward tracking returns near the original point. Tracks with more than 1.5 pixels of forward/backward disagreement are removed. <code>tracked.avi<\/code> shows displacement segments; <code>tracks.json<\/code> records surviving features and their median displacement. Points are not replenished, so the survival curve is meaningful.<\/p>\n<h2>Stress the assumptions<\/h2>\n<p>Repeat with fast motion, a lighting change or an occlusion. Explain where local brightness consistency or small-motion assumptions fail. These are image-feature trajectories, not person identities. Multi-object tracking additionally needs detections, motion prediction, association and lifecycle rules; connect those missing components to Lesson 60.<\/p>\n<h2>Evidence and mastery<\/h2>\n<p>Save one successful alignment, a parallax failure and a tracked video. Explain the difference between descriptor matching and optical flow. State what geometric or temporal information your project actually needs before choosing a more complicated model.<\/p>\n<p>References: <a href=\"https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d1/de0/tutorial_py_feature_homography.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OpenCV homography tutorial ↗<\/a> and <a href=\"https://docs.opencv.org/4.x/d4/dee/tutorial_optical_flow.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">optical flow tutorial ↗<\/a>.<\/p>"},{"id":"10","slug":"foundation","title":"Retrieve images and prompt masks","stage":"Optional extension","time":"2–4 hours","lessons":[54,55,65],"summary":"Use CLIP and SAM, then measure how wording and point prompts affect their outputs.","file":"labs/10-foundation.md","checks":["Saved CLIP rankings for two prompt wordings","Saved SAM masks for two points on the same image","Distinguished similarity/predicted quality from measured accuracy"],"html":"<p>This is an optional extension after the core training labs. It uses existing pretrained models locally. Install the additional requirements in your practical environment. The first run needs internet, several hundred MB per model and more RAM than the small CNN. CPU inference is supported but can be slow. Downloads are opt-in; omit <code>--download<\/code> once weights are cached.<\/p>\n<pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>python -m pip install -r course/practical/requirements-foundation.txt<\/code><\/pre>\n<h2>CLIP image retrieval<\/h2>\n<p>Put ten varied photographs directly in <code>course/practical/data/gallery<\/code>. Choose a query and decide which photos count as relevant before looking at rankings.<\/p>\n<pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>python -m course.practical.foundation clip --images course/practical/data/gallery --text &quot;a photo of a dog&quot; &quot;a photo of a vehicle&quot; --download --run course/practical/runs/clip-a<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>CLIP embeds text and images into a shared space. The script normalizes embeddings and ranks image/text pairs by cosine similarity. It saves <code>retrieval.json<\/code>. Scores are similarities, not probabilities of correctness. An irrelevant gallery still produces a top-ranked image.<\/p>\n<p>Repeat with two paraphrases, saving a new run. Manually label relevance and calculate precision at 3: relevant images among the first three divided by 3. Do not change the relevance definition after seeing the ranking. Include an unrelated query and compare the returned scores. Explain the contrastive objective using Lesson 54.<\/p>\n<h2>SAM point prompts<\/h2>\n<p>Choose an image and an <code>(x,y)<\/code> pixel coordinate inside the intended object. Coordinates refer to the original, EXIF-oriented image. Replace the example point if your image is smaller or the object is elsewhere.<\/p>\n<pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>python -m course.practical.foundation sam --image course/practical/data/objects.jpg --point 100 100 --download --run course/practical/runs/sam-a<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>SAM generates candidate masks for the positive point. The script selects the highest predicted-quality candidate, restores the original image dimensions, and saves a mask and overlay. Predicted quality is not a measured IoU. To measure it, add <code>--mask<\/code> pointing to an aligned binary ground-truth mask with the same dimensions; any nonzero value is foreground.<\/p>\n<h2>Experiment and mastery<\/h2>\n<p>Move the point within the same object, then onto a nearby object. Compare masks and ambiguity. A point identifies a region of interest; SAM does not automatically supply your task&#x27;s semantic class. Save two retrieval rankings, two prompted masks, your manual relevance labels and one failure of each model. Explain how prompt choice changes the task.<\/p>\n<p>References: <a href=\"https://huggingface.co/openai/clip-vit-base-patch32\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CLIP model card ↗<\/a>, <a href=\"https://huggingface.co/facebook/sam-vit-base\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SAM model card ↗<\/a> and <a href=\"https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers/v4.57.1/en/model_doc/sam\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SAM API documentation ↗<\/a>. This lab pins Transformers to version 4 compatibility because major versions can change feature-extraction APIs.<\/p>"},{"id":"11","slug":"inference","title":"Use your model on new photos","stage":"Ship and explain","time":"2–3 hours","lessons":[28,39,47],"summary":"Reload a saved classifier, preserve preprocessing and measure warm inference latency.","file":"labs/11-inference.md","checks":["Reloaded best.pt and predicted a new local photograph","Recorded latency scope, hardware and preprocessing","Documented an unknown-class or shifted-image failure"],"html":"<p>A useful training result must reload in a separate process and apply the same class order, colour convention and preprocessing. This lab packages those assumptions with a saved classifier and measures inference on a local photo.<\/p>\n<h2>Reload and predict<\/h2>\n<p>Save a new photograph as <code>course/practical/data/new-photo.jpg<\/code>, ideally of a class represented by your trained model.<\/p>\n<pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>python -m course.practical.classify predict --checkpoint course/practical/runs/cifar-small/best.pt --image course/practical/data/new-photo.jpg --repeats 30 --out course/practical/runs/new-photo-prediction.json<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The process constructs the saved architecture without fetching pretrained weights, loads its state dictionary, restores the saved resize/normalization/class mapping, switches to evaluation mode, and runs without gradient tracking. Input is converted to RGB and corrected for EXIF orientation. Only load checkpoints you created or whose source you trust; the loader uses PyTorch&#x27;s weights-only mode.<\/p>\n<p>The report gives the top three class scores, preprocessing, environment and warm median/p95 latency. Three warmup passes precede timing. CUDA runs synchronize before and after each timed pass. Timing covers batch-one forward computation and softmax; it excludes file decoding, preprocessing, model loading and output writing. That is not end-to-end request latency.<\/p>\n<h2>Measure a meaningful tradeoff<\/h2>\n<p>Compare the small CNN and your ResNet on the same image and machine. Record checkpoint size, model-only latency and validation quality. Use the same number of repeats. For an application, separately measure full request time and the data-loading bottleneck. If quantization or ONNX export becomes necessary, first verify prediction agreement with this reference implementation on representative inputs.<\/p>\n<h2>Stress the interface<\/h2>\n<p>Try a portrait-format photo, a grayscale file, a rotated-camera image and an object outside the known classes. The script always chooses among known labels; a high score on an unknown object does not establish reliability. Resizing a large photo down to the model&#x27;s input can also erase crucial detail. Record failures instead of silently changing preprocessing at inference time.<\/p>\n<h2>Write the model contract<\/h2>\n<p>Record accepted file types, RGB conversion, input size, normalization, class order, expected scene conditions, validation evidence and latency scope. Save one invocation and its JSON output so someone else can verify the artifact. A CLI is a complete local inference handoff; serving it as a web service or mobile app is a separate packaging task.<\/p>\n<h2>Evidence and mastery<\/h2>\n<p>Save predictions for three new photographs and one unknown class, plus a latency comparison. Explain why an unlabeled photo cannot measure accuracy, and why confidence thresholds must be selected using labeled validation data. Use Lab 12 to evaluate your final selected model on a held-out test set.<\/p>"},{"id":"12","slug":"capstone","title":"Build an independent CV project","stage":"Ship and explain","time":"6–10 hours","lessons":[45,46,47,48],"summary":"Choose your data and task, defend the evaluation, and hand over a reproducible result.","file":"labs/12-capstone.md","checks":["Completed the project report with fixed data and comparison protocol","Evaluated the held-out test only after choosing the model","Reproduced inference from the saved artifact and documented three failures"],"html":"<p>Choose a modest question with data you can inspect. Examples: distinguish two types of household objects across new capture sessions; count objects under changing illumination; compare a segmentation model with a threshold baseline; or align a document under viewpoint change. You do not need to use every architecture in the course.<\/p>\n<h2>Declare the protocol<\/h2>\n<p>Use the downloadable practical report template. Write the task, population, label definitions, input/output contract and baseline. Save independent groups and fixed train/validation/test IDs before training. Decide the primary metric and how you will handle empty masks, missing objects and unknown classes. State your compute budget and one planned improvement.<\/p>\n<p>Do not select the question after finding the most flattering result. If you use supplied CIFAR or PennFudan data, identify the source, subset limits and split limitations. Your own real-data project should include a data audit and annotation review.<\/p>\n<h2>Build, compare and inspect<\/h2>\n<p>Train the baseline and one improvement with declared settings. Save curves, image-level results and three failures. Choose the final procedure on validation evidence, including threshold and preprocessing decisions. Make at least one controlled comparison. If you repeat random seeds, distinguish training randomness from changes to the split.<\/p>\n<h2>Evaluate once the procedure is fixed<\/h2>\n<p>For a selected CIFAR classifier, run:<\/p>\n<pre tabindex=\"0\"><code>python -m course.practical.classify evaluate --checkpoint course/practical/runs/cifar-small/best.pt --split test --out course/practical/runs/cifar-test.json<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>This evaluates the full official test portion unless you explicitly request <code>--per-class<\/code>; record any subset. For your own data, add <code>--manifest course/practical/data/my-split.json<\/code>. Detection and segmentation have equivalent explicit test commands in their labs. If you change the method after inspecting test failures, describe that test as development data and obtain new held-out evidence for the revised method.<\/p>\n<h2>Hand over the result<\/h2>\n<p>Include the runnable command, environment snapshot, data provenance and split, saved artifact, model contract, primary metric with sample counts, baseline comparison, latency if relevant and a failure gallery. Reproduce one inference result from a fresh process. Keep private photos and large checkpoints out of the Git repository; explain how an authorized collaborator obtains them.<\/p>\n<h2>Mastery gate<\/h2>\n<p>Ask yourself: Can I justify the split? Trace one example through preprocessing and output? Explain the loss and metric? Show a baseline? Reproduce the saved result? Explain three failures without inventing causes? State the population the evidence supports?<\/p>\n<p>If one answer is no, revisit the relevant lab. Finishing a command is a useful checkpoint; completing this evidence is what closes the practical learning gap. Further specialties such as robotics/SLAM, medical imaging, OCR, large-scale training or production serving can become separate projects after this foundation.<\/p>"}];
